S8B — Execution interfaces and the deleted-code rehearsal
========================================================================

Status: execution preparation and review correction; product qualification remains open.
Parent: S8A b573284cb4587d4fb0085cc69a80a036830f1b76.
The linked s8-consumer-readiness.tsv remains the consumer tracker; this record
records B's public-interface decisions. Canonical configuration, document and
interactive compilation, mixed compute partitioning, retained import binding,
and bundle provider planning now have implementation witnesses. Document input/initializer/static-symbol methods are now implemented. The complete
configured product matrix and deleted-code qualification below still gate acceptance.
A removal row marked routed has switched its caller to canonical input and must
have zero old parser calls. It remains open for deletion qualification and cannot
satisfy a demonstrated consumer's dependency gate.

1. Compiler migration contract
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Replace the Program input in ProgramCompiler and ProgramCompilerView with the
retained SourceDocument / its selected typed document owner. Preserve each
existing method's output and host boundary. Prepared interfaces (shipping tree-route removal remains C-owned):

compile_tree -> compile_document -> ProgramCompilationProduct.
compile_interactive_tree -> compile_interactive_document -> observable product.
compile_tree_artifact -> compile_document_artifact -> ProgramArtifact product.
compile_tree_artifact_with_inputs -> compile_document_artifact_with_inputs:
  retain detached planning values and the explicit set of live input names.
compile_tree_artifact_with_input_initializers -> corresponding document method:
  capture declaration-time values in the same short-lived planning pass; do not
  run a second evaluator or leak compiler cells into resident state. Canonical
  initialization and live projections share one retained document and one resource
  planning context; only the initialization projection is temporarily activated.
  That projection retains requested defaults and their canonical dependencies,
  including state writers and integrity constraints. Unrelated document outputs
  remain in the executable artifact and are not evaluated to capture defaults.
evaluate_static_tree_symbols[_with_inputs] -> document variants:
  preserve requested-symbol filtering and detached RuntimeHostInputValue outputs.
  Requested symbols have distinct output identities, including a variable named
  result, and canonical matrix snapshots retain logical row-major order.
compile_mixed_tree -> compile_mixed_document:
  canonical typed declaration/executable partitions retain module resolution,
  compute regions, coordinator artifacts and advertised target requirements.

Source text methods construct the retained source once and delegate to these
interfaces. Root/resolved-source methods reuse the cache owner. Do not construct
Program to call an unchanged method. Do not insert a parser flag into a compiler.
Missing lowering/resolution semantics block the owning S4/S7A prerequisite.

Initializers and planning values are not automatically live input ports. The
external_input_names distinction is an acceptance assertion, not optional cleanup.
Resource-derived defaults are detached from the live graph; configured resource
reads remain live only where executable bindings still use them. Local functions
can introduce a configured resource read on first use while ordinary caller
bindings remain outside their local scope. Canonical tuple destructuring binds
local function outputs through the maintained tuple-selection operations.
Constant ranges use the shared cardinality authority for fixed shape inference;
runtime-bound ranges retain independent dimension parameters. Matrix annotations
without extents infer independent row and column dimensions through the shared
type solver. Nested matrices retain separate dimensions. Supplied planning
values specialize annotations and rebind snapshots against the destination's
shape context; incompatible element kinds still reject. Repeated input
annotations share constraints regardless of occurrence order. Typed module
imports bind catalog exports for wildcard, item, group and alias forms, and mixed
compute regions inherit root imports without sharing local variable bindings.
Resolved host capabilities and module catalog ownership stay in the current
ProgramCompiler boundary. Production witnesses must enter those methods.

Compute state initialization walks only the canonical artifact's activation
dependency graph and memoizes shared values once per compilation. Both compute
lowerers consume the same row-major initializer snapshots; the fixed-shape target
converts them to its column-major storage layout. Arithmetic uses the shared
compute operation mapping and range construction uses core's canonical visitor.
Activation admits declared pure signal operations with independent whole-value
writes. Live inputs, recurrence state, cycles and unresolved extents reject before
session creation. No recurrence turn runs to obtain declaration-time values.
Qualification covers shared matrix initializers, subsequent turns, and the shipped
particle initialization at 5, 257, 16384 and 1000000 particles. The early browser
canary uses computed range/remainder/concatenation initializers.

Interactive document compilation shares the ordinary compiler's configured
resource schemas, bindings and external-contract resolver. Only output visibility
differs. Planning reads snapshots and prepares no live reads or effects;
authorization remains part of runtime admission. A denied interactive candidate
leaves the accepted source and state intact.
Typed and finalized-stream submissions share selection preparation, suppression,
source echo and candidate commit. Finality is checked before preparation. The
canonical interactive compiler binds synthetic ans to the preceding source value;
it is neither a live port nor recurrence state. Failed submissions preserve the
pending selection, and successful submissions consume it exactly once.

Fixed-shape compute retains derived output publications separately from recurrence
state in its backend-neutral storage plan. Each derived slot has one publication
buffer even when several output names alias it. Publications begin zeroed, commit
with the accepted candidate, and preserve their prior contents after an integrity
rejection. CPU scalar, SIMD, JIT, native GPU and browser GPU use the same physical
double-buffer plan; only recurrence state is loaded into the next turn's registers.
Publication-only computations remain live during instruction pruning. Fixed-shape
elementwise broadcasting projects each matrix axis before indexing its physical
column-major registers; both row and column inputs retain their logical order.
The canonical compute browser canary checks this source-to-WASM interface against
both CPU and real WebGPU after each WASM build, before the native server build.
The browser creates an explicit pipeline layout from the physical plan so shader
reflection cannot drop an unused publication read binding. Full application and
deleted-code canaries remain required for acceptance.

2. Bundle/browser decisions requiring implementation before C approval
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The old producer serializes nodes::Program into CODE; WasmDocument/bootstrap
loaders decode it. That payload must be removed at both boundaries together.
Prepare retained source plus current accepted artifact publication and loader
admission. Keep the existing bytecode/artifact format. Preserve source-resolution
edges, source identity, HTML/output binding identities and browser target checks.

CanonicalProgramBundle is the prepared source/artifact envelope. Version 2 binds
all resolved transitive dependency source hashes collected by the root compiler.
The browser checks those identities against its supplied source map before loading
bytecode; missing or changed dependencies and older envelopes require regeneration.
The bytecode/artifact format is unchanged. The native
producer/load witness executes a generated three-document import chain, including
configured resource planning in dependencies. The documented browser DOM source
bundles with four assignment effects. Bundle linking shares the file resolver's
exact/appended-extension/directory-index candidates. HTML formats the admitted
canonical syntax directly. Only configured run roots receive executable artifacts;
served prose and dependency documents retain source and HTML without requiring
independent activation. The real static bootstrap validates each root artifact
and accepts non-root sources without artifact URLs. Browser distribution acceptance remains
open until the complete matrix executes after C removes the retiring code.
Old AST payloads must reject with a regeneration instruction. No format-tree
reconstruction or old decoder fallback. Native/WASM version/target admission
continues through existing artifact validation.

3. First C rehearsal and ownership of expected failures
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Create the actual codex/syntax-s8c-cutover branch now. Begin by physically removing
the Nom recognizer/direct-rule modules and document/lower/legacy, and draft CLI
classification's canonical route. Keep all maintained tests until their reviewed
disposition is implemented; unretargeted tests are blockers, not passes/skips.

A owns preparation of source-cache, resolver, presentation and retained-source
interfaces for its ten contracts. B owns compiler/config/interactive/bundle/browser
interface preparation for its seventeen execution contracts.
S7A owns missing renderer/declaration behavior; S4 owns semantic implementation.
Only C changes shipping routes, module/export wiring and dependency removal after
the owning A/B interface is prepared. A route failure caused by an interface gap
returns to that interface owner; the final route change stays with C. No new
semantic layer belongs on C.

The first draft is deliberately incomplete and cannot satisfy deletion closure:
remaining product routes and AST owners must still be migrated. Record its exact
compiler errors, not a blanket expected-red exemption. A/B cannot be accepted
until the complete C product matrix passes with those dependencies removed.

The canonical library compiling after deletion is only an early boundary probe.
It is not CLI/runtime/browser acceptance. Final evidence must include actual
product entry points and all named distributions from syntax-s8-cutover-plan.mec.

Rooted interactive and resolved revisions
----------------------------------------

The canonical root compiler now shares one graph implementation for ordinary
and interactive projections. `compile_canonical_interactive_root` publishes live
root symbols with the same configured resources and detached dependency exports.
`compile_canonical_resolved_root` and its interactive counterpart consume the
supplied retained root revision without resolving that root a second time.

`canonical_resolved_and_rooted_interactive_compilation_preserve_revision_and_symbols`
executes ordinary and interactive rooted/resolved interfaces, both with and without
module-build options: a previously resolved revision produces 42,
while a later resolver revision produces 100. Both retain dependency identity;
only interactive products publish the answer symbol. Resource requirements,
missing dependencies, and compiler reuse after rejection are covered by
`canonical_interactive_root_keeps_resource_authority_and_dependency_errors`.
The canonical runtime regression set passes 57 tests. The option-taking variants
use the existing ModuleBuilder for target, edition, feature, capability and
transitive module identities in the same resolution pass. No module records are
persisted into a runtime store. The reduced full_source/resident-routing-source
profile also compiles. Ordered multi-root compilation remains open; these prepared entry points do not
replace that responsibility.


Rooted mixed compilation
------------------------

`compile_canonical_mixed_root` and `compile_canonical_mixed_resolved_root` now use
the same dependency-resolution session and ModuleBuilder identity authority as
ordinary roots. Resolved namespaces and detached exports enter the existing
coordinator, compute and initialization projections. Both products retain the
same transitive source hashes. No declaration tree or reconstructed source is
used by these interfaces.

`canonical_rooted_mixed_compilation_shares_transitive_imports_and_initializers`
covers a two-edge import chain consumed by both coordinator and compute. It
checks the scalar default (3), captured activation input (6), and two completed
compute turns (9 and 13), plus cycle rejection and compiler reuse afterward.
The canonical runtime regression set passes 58 tests. Full served mixed browser
adoption and the final C product matrix remain separate open acceptance work.

Interactive state identity and replacement
------------------------------------------

Canonical interactive bindings now name retained mutable cells and publish the
implicit `ans` alias explicitly. Projection refresh derives a state writer's
final candidate value from its published cell instead of executing that transition
again. Activation and queries share implicit-result selection while preserving
the ordinary output's name. This uses existing artifact identities and execution
storage; it introduces no source replay or alternate evaluator.

`canonical_interactive_turns_and_replacement_preserve_live_state` checks three
accepted turns followed by a replacement that retains state 3 and displays 13.
`canonical_interactive_matrix_projection_and_failed_candidate_preserve_state`
covers a two-row matrix, rejection rollback and the following turn.
`canonical_interactive_rewired_projection_uses_the_migrated_epoch` distinguishes
two live state identities and checks a changed output dependency.
`canonical_interactive_result_identity_is_independent_of_fence_publication`
checks a final result different from the preceding formatted fence.
The full B runtime library profile with compute executes 709 tests, all passing.


5. Fixed closeout sequence and evidence rules (2026-09-15)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is the completion sequence for the existing review, not a new milestone or
an expansion of the language. The consumer census remains the seventeen B rows
in grammar-audit/s8-consumer-readiness.tsv. Required semantic behavior is bounded
by the maintained source/type/operation contracts and checked-in applications.
A passing retiring compiler, isolated parser rule, or extracted compute region
cannot qualify a complete canonical product. Existing component evidence remains
useful, but its narrower scope must be stated.

Proceed in this order, repairing concrete failures in their shared owner:

1. Audit types and operations before further architectural implementation. Use
   BuiltinScalarKind::ALL (17 kinds) and SchemaBody as the census, and the existing
   phase-2i-semantic-completion.tsv for explicit target capability dispositions.
   Exercise retained complete documents, artifact encode/decode, live changes,
   downstream propagation, and rejection/rollback where the contract applies.
   Scalar coverage does not imply matrix, composite, or backend coverage.
2. Close the known full runtime failures in the one deleted-parser C candidate:
   terminal logical assignments; ordinary result identity when presentation repeats
   it; comprehension-backed computed initialization; maintained pattern-function
   execution. Preserve application values, constraints, recurrence and rollback.
   Ordered roots and resource planning are implemented and have paired witnesses.
3. Complete the actual canonical WasmDocument transport and configured served
   activation. Qualify the full particle and EKF applications and stale dependency
   rejection at the real browser loader. Kernel-only and envelope-helper results
   do not satisfy this gate.
4. Reconcile each of the original seventeen B consumer rows with its switched C
   caller, positive/negative tests, feature profile, exact candidate and result.
   Unretargeted tests, zero-test runs and required skipped adapters are blockers.
5. Freeze the implementation, run the complete deleted-code product/distribution
   matrix and affected CPU/SIMD/JIT/GPU/memory profiles, address every relevant
   review note, then obtain a clean review and green exact-head CI. Re-run affected
   checks after a correction; do not add optional cleanup or new language features.

R26 physical publication correction, 2026-10-03
----------------------------------------------

The correction starts from R26 `3f1f23772a588c980c2a84c3acc5bb73ecc60fee`
on R06 `e5101287f1c2436d6fdb8a00566274f92ba3d0d0`. Review 5402937177
identified that the direct-output selector regression did not cover ordinary
tuple publications. A source-level `(selected, selector)` reproduction failed
before the fix with unsupported `range/inclusive` lowering; that nonempty test
passed after the correction.

The batch compiler now obtains the existing compute interface before selector
classification and retains that same interface for final publication storage.
Its physical output leaves already resolve aliases and recursively expand
composite packs. Selector retention uses those leaves, not a second incomplete
publication traversal. A pack carrying a published selector is a structural
consumer; the selector remains admitted, materialized and published. Existing
range, scalar-instruction and selector-source limits remain unchanged.

The added source/decoded regression exercises all four inclusive/exclusive range
forms with and without increments. Ordinary tuples, nested tuples, repeated
selector publications and a directly published input run across two batch lanes.
Assertions cover complete interface equality, selector 1x2 and selected-value
2x1 geometry, one physical buffer for repeated publications, exact selector and
selected values, and input publication. It constructs normal canonical source
artifacts rather than adding a manual OutputDeclaration to avoid tuple packing.

