- `mech --no-default-features --features
  full_source,bundle_web,formatter,serve,compute_backends_native --lib`:
  303 passed, one environment-dependent actual-package test separately executed.
  The native server lifecycle check used a permitted temporary loopback port.
- `scripts/tests/static-project.test.mjs`: 6 passed.
- CI impact/full-contract Python suites: 75 passed.
- Actual `browser_project` package built with wasm-pack for wasm32, then exercised
  by the publisher: native-supported `math/fmod` is rejected by this narrower
  package before creating output; a compatible enum/u8/i32-source retry succeeds.
  The ignored-by-default actual-package case ran and passed separately, using
  the final native test executable. Full CI now selects it while the freshly
  built browser package is still present.
- Actual browser admission matches actual source loading for the checked scalar
  cases. Valid u8/i32 annotations are accepted by this package; invalid literal
  spellings are not used as evidence of a feature gap. A console-writing source
  passes the compiler probe without logging an effect.
- Maintained `browser_compute_canary` wasm32 check passed. An ad-hoc
  `browser_project_core,browser_compute` native combination remains unqualified:
  existing compare macro warnings in its incomplete vector feature closure are
  not suppressed or repaired as part of this patch.
- Canonical evidence imports, warning policy, compiler-planning quarantine,
  runtime-factory safety, production resident routing, interactive architecture,
  retired-value-system absence, formatting and whitespace checks passed.

The initial broader native run exposed stale server tests decoding retained
source as a native artifact, an empty compute-host configuration, missing
controller setup, and CLI mocks lacking the new target API. Those fixtures now
assert the current retained-source contract, exact dependency routes/resolutions,
native execution and valid configured hosts. The final 303-test run is green;
initial failed runs are not counted as qualification.

Four deterministic negative controls were executed and restored:

- Removing nominal provenance delivery fails the exact bootstrap provenance test.
- Omitting pointer listener installation fails the normalized-sample test.
- Disabling literal comment fallback fails the physical-line test (`-- use { here`
  stops at byte 7 instead of byte 13).
- Bypassing the real target compiler lets missing `math/fmod` escape admission
  and fails the actual-package refusal assertion. Restored admission rejects it.

Each mutation ran a nonempty decisive case. Restored Node, syntax and real-package
checks pass. These suite counts overlap and are not added into a coverage total.
C is not sealed. CI remains paused; the published correcting head is submitted
for review before returning to the outstanding R26 review finding.

S8C static mixed loading and live ingress correction, 2026-10-04
--------------------------------------------------------------

The correcting implementation/test candidate is
`433e159345c623e38a9bd0f4bb8cbda3a87f639a`, on R15
`7b197ca5fbec577ecb3125c305219297d844d420`, R26
`0f1c8feabf87a0bb86f9a986d3e6329639ff8ee8`, and sealed R06
`e5101287f1c2436d6fdb8a00566274f92ba3d0d0`. All 13 C patches through
`4a936c78aa03388a2eb59a03f37273859dc9ab1b` have identical range-diff
entries across the restack. The issued-settings correction in `29d116413`
is an additional implementation delta, not an identical-tree restack.
`433e15934` changes only two wasm-target fixture arguments; the production
correction and native qualification remain at `29d116413`.

Codex review 5403855830 applies to the previous published C head
`0f7f327ac969de4e52471b8f0060b19b81250bae`. Its P2 comment 4175773279
and P1 comment 4175773281 are corrected at their shared lifecycle and
preparation owners:

- Served pointer events query the accepted running input driver. A configured
  but unused host does not submit events; replacing source to remove or restore
  the last read updates readiness without stale button/time state. Stop and
  encoded reset retain driver ownership. Static pointer readiness is preserved.
- Static admission and public source constructors reuse the existing mixed-root
  preparation, compute factory and coordinator. The admission factory does not
  create a device, activate a runtime, advance a turn, start drivers or deliver
  effects. Retained imports, nominal identity, issued authority, runtime limits
  and grants remain in the existing owners.
- The static bundle emits the existing browser-compute bridge unchanged. Its
  client uses Device/Session ownership, exact completion and pending-command
  gating; refusal never replays an input. Stop retires a pending session, and
  unload during device creation disposes the late resource. Automatic CPU
  reconstruction is limited to pre-start device failure, not a submitted turn.

A related concrete mismatch was corrected in both served and static preparation:
backend/manifest selection now uses the issued compute settings that the runtime
uses. Legitimate CPU/WGPU/auto overrides work without changing roots, grants or
host identity; a settings/compiled-region mismatch is still rejected by the
factory. This is not a new evaluator, catalog expansion or transaction redesign.

The original served pointer handler fails the inactive-driver regression against
`0f7f327ac9`, while the corrected handler passes. The final 16-case static client
suite has eight passing and eight failing cases against that reviewed source,
and all 16 pass after restoration. These tests use real Session ownership with
mocked device resources, not actual browser GPU qualification. Native tests
assert the exact original `RuntimeHostProviderNotFound` and missing-compute
provider message; issued-settings tests execute the original source-only backend
negative control and assert `ComputeHostConfiguration` on a region mismatch.

Two initial new execution fixtures incorrectly expected an initialized derived
sample or attempted manual advancement of an external-input program. The
runtime correctly leaves those samples dormant and rejects manual advancement.
The fixtures now start the registered pointer driver and execute only through
admitted host input. No runtime allowance or hidden preparation dispatch was
added. An earlier broad canary run also selected an nbody case outside that
profile's combinatorics catalog. None of those failed attempts is a passing
qualification result.

The first real Chrome constructor attempt executed one case and correctly
rejected a fixture's null source-resolution value. The production static client
already supplies an array. `433e15934` supplies explicit empty arrays in the two
new browser fixtures without relaxing the public API; that failed attempt is
not a browser pass.

